course: dictionary
lesson: 8871
title: Shorten: make it smaller
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: To shorten something is to make it shorter. It can mean shorter in length, like «shortening trousers», shorter in time, like a lesson, or a shorter name, like «Benjamin» made into «Ben».
explain: Look at what is being shortened to work out which meaning fits. A tailor shortens trousers, so they get shorter in length. A lesson that is shortened lasts less time. Kipling shortens «Taffimai Metallumai» to «Taffy», so the name is made smaller.
explain: Check your meaning by putting it into the sentence. If you read «The teacher shortened the lesson» and think it means made the lesson last less time, the sentence still makes sense, so you have it right.
fact: To shorten means to make something shorter.
fact: «Info» is a shortened form of «information», so it is a smaller version of the word.
fact: Text speak shortens words with letters and numbers that sound like them: «l8r» means «later» and «m8» means «mate».
fact: Days shorten in autumn, which means they get shorter in time.
fact: Shorten is a verb: shorten, shortens, shortened, shortening.
exercise: «A tailor will shorten the trousers.» What does shorten mean here? | Make them last less time | Make them longer | *Make them shorter in length
exercise: Kipling shortens «Taffimai Metallumai» to «Taffy». What is the shortened name? | Hyphen | Taffimai Metallumai | *Taffy | Stone Age
exercise: «Info» is a shortened form of which word? | *information | infinity | inflate | infant
test: «The teacher shortened the lesson because of the fire drill.» What does shortened mean here? | *Made it last less time | Made it longer | Made it louder
test: «Text speak shortens words, so u means you.» Why is u a shortened word? | It is a number | It is a long word | *It is shorter, using a letter that sounds like a word
exam: «The biceps shortens to bend the elbow.» What is the muscle doing? | Turning the arm round | Growing bigger | *Getting shorter, which pulls the arm to bend | Stretching the arm out straight
